AUDIO: 'For our prompt, the general Minimax H3 prompting guide works well. The creator of the Minimax H3 mod nodes also has a much more in-depth guide on how to prompt. In this case, we\'re just going with a simple prompt. While this is'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'running, I thought it would be good to actually describe the difference between a ref mod and a LoRA. A LoRA works by training on a set of images or videos and learning small changes to the model\'s weights. Those learned changes are saved into a lightweight file that'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can later be applied to recreate a character, style, or concept. A ref mod takes a different approach. Instead of training the model, it runs your reference images or videos through Minimax H3\'s VAE and converts them into latent representations. Those reference'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'latents are then saved into a small file that can be reused during generation. That also makes ref mods dramatically faster to create. Training a LoRA can take hours depending on the dataset, hardware, and settings. Creating a ref'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mod mostly involves encoding the references, so it can often be produced in seconds or minutes instead. The files can also be much smaller. A video model LoRA can easily be tens or hundreds of megabytes, while ref mods can be only a'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'few megabytes. So, while a LoRA teaches the model something new by modifying its weights, a ref mod essentially saves a reusable representation of your references in H3\'s latent space. It is faster to create, much smaller, and'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'doesn\'t require a traditional training process. All right. Now, let\'s have a look at the finished video. &gt;&gt; This is demonstration of the EIE Minimax H3 ref mod. &gt;&gt; So, it gets the identity down well, but'

